WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
Quality control helps determine whether
materials and products meet approved quality

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.610
requirements. It includes
appropriate sampling,

3
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:12.680
specifications, testing,
documentation and review. Quality

4
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:17.160
cannot be assured by final testing
alone, so laboratory controls work

5
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.270
alongside manufacturing
controls. Quality control

6
00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:23.160
activities should be
independent where required

7
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.070
by the applicable quality
system and regulations.

8
00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:32.350
A specification describes tests, analytical
procedures and acceptance criteria for a

9
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:36.650
material or product. Approved methods
should be suitable for their intended

10
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:42.690
use, with validation or verification as
appropriate. Analysts must use current

11
00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.770
controlled procedures and suitable reference
standards. Results should be compared against

12
00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:52.480
the actual approved specification,
not an assumed industry-wide number.

13
00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:58.840
Sampling plans should consider the
material, process, risk and intended test.

14
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.800
Samples must be appropriately identified,
collected, handled and stored to preserve

15
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.480
integrity. A result from a poorly
selected or compromised sample may

16
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.600
not represent the batch. Sampling procedures
and any required environmental controls

17
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.240
should be defined and followed.
Traceability links each sample to its

18
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.540
source and analysis.

19
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:26.940
Laboratory instruments must be suitable for their
intended measurements. Qualification, calibration,

20
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.740
maintenance and system suitability
checks may be needed depending on the

21
00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:35.960
equipment and method. Analysts should
verify instrument status before use and

22
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.850
preserve relevant records.
A successful system

23
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.180
suitability check does not automatically

24
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.350
excuse overdue calibration or
missing qualification evidence.

25
00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:51.110
An out-of-specification result,
often called OOS, occurs when a

26
00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.550
result falls outside an
established acceptance

27
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:57.450
criterion. Such results require timely,

28
00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:01.110
documented evaluation under
applicable procedures.

29
00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.130
A laboratory investigation
may examine potential

30
00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:06.770
analytical errors, while
the wider assessment

31
00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:09.750
considers manufacturing and product impact

32
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:16.010
where appropriate. Retesting must follow
justified approved procedures and must not

33
00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.430
be used simply to obtain a passing result.

34
00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:22.450
Laboratory data must be
attributable, legible,

35
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.330
contemporaneous, original
and accurate, with

36
00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:29.090
additional expectations for
completeness, consistency,

37
00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:32.530
endurance and availability.
Electronic raw data,

38
00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.790
audit trails, calculations and relevant
metadata should be controlled according to

39
00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.990
risk and applicable
requirements. Transcribing

40
00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.190
a final number without retaining required

41
00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.710
underlying data may undermine
traceability and review.

42
00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:51.530
Batch disposition decisions rely on
more than a passing laboratory result.

43
00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:58.950
Authorized personnel should evaluate relevant manufacturing
records, deviations, testing results, specifications

44
00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:04.750
and other required evidence. Responsibilities
and legal release requirements vary by

45
00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:10.030
jurisdiction and product. A laboratory
analyst should not independently declare a

46
00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:13.250
batch releasable merely
because a single test passed.

47
00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.030
Imagine a finished-product
assay result outside

48
00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.510
its approved specification. The analyst

49
00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:23.430
should preserve the original data and
promptly report the result according

50
00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:28.710
to the laboratory procedure. Authorized personnel
should conduct an appropriate investigation

51
00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:33.990
and assess possible batch impact.
The result must not be deleted,

52
00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:38.870
averaged away or replaced by an
unjustified repeat test. The documented

53
00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.880
outcome informs the
final quality decision.
